$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 02/Practice
::HC-W2-P01::Adults say hypertension screening is unnecessary because they feel healthy, even though screening is nearby and free. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=perceived susceptibility and asymptomatic risk#Correct. The dominant barrier is a belief about personal risk, not physical access.
~subjective norms and social opportunity#Not the best answer. The dominant barrier is a belief about personal risk, not physical access.
~self-efficacy#Not the best answer. The dominant barrier is a belief about personal risk, not physical access.
~physical opportunity in COM-B#Not the best answer. The dominant barrier is a belief about personal risk, not physical access.
}

::HC-W2-P02::Adults say hypertension screening is unnecessary because they feel healthy, even though screening is nearby and free. What is the strongest next response? {
=Explain that hypertension can be present without symptoms and link that belief to a convenient screening action.#Correct. The dominant barrier is a belief about personal risk, not physical access.
~Engage household decision-makers while preserving women’s autonomy and address transport decision constraints.#Not the best answer. The dominant barrier is a belief about personal risk, not physical access.
~Use demonstration, guided practice and teach-back so caregivers build confidence performing the preparation.#Not the best answer. The dominant barrier is a belief about personal risk, not physical access.
~Address net availability or distribution first, while communication supports correct allocation and use.#Not the best answer. The dominant barrier is a belief about personal risk, not physical access.
}

::HC-W2-P03::Mothers know the benefits of childhood vaccination but report that partners decide whether transport money can be used for clinic visits. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=subjective norms and social opportunity#Correct. Knowledge is high; social influence and opportunity constrain action.
~self-efficacy#Not the best answer. Knowledge is high; social influence and opportunity constrain action.
~physical opportunity in COM-B#Not the best answer. Knowledge is high; social influence and opportunity constrain action.
~relative advantage in diffusion of innovations#Not the best answer. Knowledge is high; social influence and opportunity constrain action.
}

::HC-W2-P04::Mothers know the benefits of childhood vaccination but report that partners decide whether transport money can be used for clinic visits. What is the strongest next response? {
=Engage household decision-makers while preserving women’s autonomy and address transport decision constraints.#Correct. Knowledge is high; social influence and opportunity constrain action.
~Use demonstration, guided practice and teach-back so caregivers build confidence performing the preparation.#Not the best answer. Knowledge is high; social influence and opportunity constrain action.
~Address net availability or distribution first, while communication supports correct allocation and use.#Not the best answer. Knowledge is high; social influence and opportunity constrain action.
~Emphasise the verified advantages while giving clear instructions and referral pathways for reactive results.#Not the best answer. Knowledge is high; social influence and opportunity constrain action.
}

::HC-W2-P05::Community health workers can explain oral rehydration correctly but caregivers say they are afraid they will mix it wrongly at home. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=self-efficacy#Correct. Confidence in capability is the salient modifiable mechanism.
~physical opportunity in COM-B#Not the best answer. Confidence in capability is the salient modifiable mechanism.
~relative advantage in diffusion of innovations#Not the best answer. Confidence in capability is the salient modifiable mechanism.
~subjective norm#Not the best answer. Confidence in capability is the salient modifiable mechanism.
}

::HC-W2-P06::Community health workers can explain oral rehydration correctly but caregivers say they are afraid they will mix it wrongly at home. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use demonstration, guided practice and teach-back so caregivers build confidence performing the preparation.#Correct. Confidence in capability is the salient modifiable mechanism.
~Address net availability or distribution first, while communication supports correct allocation and use.#Not the best answer. Confidence in capability is the salient modifiable mechanism.
~Emphasise the verified advantages while giving clear instructions and referral pathways for reactive results.#Not the best answer. Confidence in capability is the salient modifiable mechanism.
~Use peer-informed strategies and confidential access rather than only repeating individual risk messages.#Not the best answer. Confidence in capability is the salient modifiable mechanism.
}

::HC-W2-P07::Residents want to use mosquito nets but many households do not own enough nets for all sleeping spaces. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=physical opportunity in COM-B#Correct. Motivation cannot compensate for missing resources.
~relative advantage in diffusion of innovations#Not the best answer. Motivation cannot compensate for missing resources.
~subjective norm#Not the best answer. Motivation cannot compensate for missing resources.
~physical opportunity and structural constraint#Not the best answer. Motivation cannot compensate for missing resources.
}

::HC-W2-P08::Residents want to use mosquito nets but many households do not own enough nets for all sleeping spaces. What is the strongest next response? {
=Address net availability or distribution first, while communication supports correct allocation and use.#Correct. Motivation cannot compensate for missing resources.
~Emphasise the verified advantages while giving clear instructions and referral pathways for reactive results.#Not the best answer. Motivation cannot compensate for missing resources.
~Use peer-informed strategies and confidential access rather than only repeating individual risk messages.#Not the best answer. Motivation cannot compensate for missing resources.
~Redesign appointment flow or service hours; do not frame non-attendance as an awareness failure.#Not the best answer. Motivation cannot compensate for missing resources.
}
$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 02/Mastery
::HC-W2-M01::Men attending a workplace clinic say HIV self-testing feels private and convenient compared with facility testing. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=relative advantage in diffusion of innovations#Correct. Adoption is helped when the innovation offers a meaningful advantage over current practice.
~subjective norm#Not the best answer. Adoption is helped when the innovation offers a meaningful advantage over current practice.
~physical opportunity and structural constraint#Not the best answer. Adoption is helped when the innovation offers a meaningful advantage over current practice.
~perceived complexity and capability#Not the best answer. Adoption is helped when the innovation offers a meaningful advantage over current practice.
}

::HC-W2-M02::Men attending a workplace clinic say HIV self-testing feels private and convenient compared with facility testing. What is the strongest next response? {
=Emphasise the verified advantages while giving clear instructions and referral pathways for reactive results.#Correct. Adoption is helped when the innovation offers a meaningful advantage over current practice.
~Use peer-informed strategies and confidential access rather than only repeating individual risk messages.#Not the best answer. Adoption is helped when the innovation offers a meaningful advantage over current practice.
~Redesign appointment flow or service hours; do not frame non-attendance as an awareness failure.#Not the best answer. Adoption is helped when the innovation offers a meaningful advantage over current practice.
~Simplify the behaviour into feasible steps and demonstrate locally available meal options.#Not the best answer. Adoption is helped when the innovation offers a meaningful advantage over current practice.
}

::HC-W2-M03::Adolescents report that their friends would mock anyone seen entering the youth-friendly clinic. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=subjective norm#Correct. Perceived peer approval can strongly shape intention.
~physical opportunity and structural constraint#Not the best answer. Perceived peer approval can strongly shape intention.
~perceived complexity and capability#Not the best answer. Perceived peer approval can strongly shape intention.
~observational learning#Not the best answer. Perceived peer approval can strongly shape intention.
}

::HC-W2-M04::Adolescents report that their friends would mock anyone seen entering the youth-friendly clinic. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use peer-informed strategies and confidential access rather than only repeating individual risk messages.#Correct. Perceived peer approval can strongly shape intention.
~Redesign appointment flow or service hours; do not frame non-attendance as an awareness failure.#Not the best answer. Perceived peer approval can strongly shape intention.
~Simplify the behaviour into feasible steps and demonstrate locally available meal options.#Not the best answer. Perceived peer approval can strongly shape intention.
~Use credible peer modelling and opportunities for workers to try the behaviour themselves.#Not the best answer. Perceived peer approval can strongly shape intention.
}

::HC-W2-M05::Women intend to attend antenatal care, but the clinic queue routinely consumes an entire workday. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=physical opportunity and structural constraint#Correct. The principal barrier is service opportunity, which messaging alone cannot remove.
~perceived complexity and capability#Not the best answer. The principal barrier is service opportunity, which messaging alone cannot remove.
~observational learning#Not the best answer. The principal barrier is service opportunity, which messaging alone cannot remove.
~opportunity rather than motivation#Not the best answer. The principal barrier is service opportunity, which messaging alone cannot remove.
}

::HC-W2-M06::Women intend to attend antenatal care, but the clinic queue routinely consumes an entire workday. What is the strongest next response? {
=Redesign appointment flow or service hours; do not frame non-attendance as an awareness failure.#Correct. The principal barrier is service opportunity, which messaging alone cannot remove.
~Simplify the behaviour into feasible steps and demonstrate locally available meal options.#Not the best answer. The principal barrier is service opportunity, which messaging alone cannot remove.
~Use credible peer modelling and opportunities for workers to try the behaviour themselves.#Not the best answer. The principal barrier is service opportunity, which messaging alone cannot remove.
~Investigate transport support, decentralised refill options or community delivery before intensifying motivational messaging.#Not the best answer. The principal barrier is service opportunity, which messaging alone cannot remove.
}

::HC-W2-M07::A diabetes programme teaches dietary advice but participants say healthy meals seem too complex to prepare. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=perceived complexity and capability#Correct. Complexity can reduce adoption and perceived capability.
~observational learning#Not the best answer. Complexity can reduce adoption and perceived capability.
~opportunity rather than motivation#Not the best answer. Complexity can reduce adoption and perceived capability.
~compatibility in diffusion of innovations#Not the best answer. Complexity can reduce adoption and perceived capability.
}

::HC-W2-M08::A diabetes programme teaches dietary advice but participants say healthy meals seem too complex to prepare. What is the strongest next response? {
=Simplify the behaviour into feasible steps and demonstrate locally available meal options.#Correct. Complexity can reduce adoption and perceived capability.
~Use credible peer modelling and opportunities for workers to try the behaviour themselves.#Not the best answer. Complexity can reduce adoption and perceived capability.
~Investigate transport support, decentralised refill options or community delivery before intensifying motivational messaging.#Not the best answer. Complexity can reduce adoption and perceived capability.
~Explain how the new recommendation fits with familiar routines and give a simple schedule comparison.#Not the best answer. Complexity can reduce adoption and perceived capability.
}

::HC-W2-M09::Farmers begin wearing protective gloves after seeing respected peers demonstrate their use without slowing work. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=observational learning#Correct. Seeing similar others perform the behaviour successfully can strengthen self-efficacy and outcome expectations.
~opportunity rather than motivation#Not the best answer. Seeing similar others perform the behaviour successfully can strengthen self-efficacy and outcome expectations.
~compatibility in diffusion of innovations#Not the best answer. Seeing similar others perform the behaviour successfully can strengthen self-efficacy and outcome expectations.
~perceived behavioural control#Not the best answer. Seeing similar others perform the behaviour successfully can strengthen self-efficacy and outcome expectations.
}

::HC-W2-M10::Farmers begin wearing protective gloves after seeing respected peers demonstrate their use without slowing work. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use credible peer modelling and opportunities for workers to try the behaviour themselves.#Correct. Seeing similar others perform the behaviour successfully can strengthen self-efficacy and outcome expectations.
~Investigate transport support, decentralised refill options or community delivery before intensifying motivational messaging.#Not the best answer. Seeing similar others perform the behaviour successfully can strengthen self-efficacy and outcome expectations.
~Explain how the new recommendation fits with familiar routines and give a simple schedule comparison.#Not the best answer. Seeing similar others perform the behaviour successfully can strengthen self-efficacy and outcome expectations.
~Add negotiation skills, partner-level approaches and supportive services rather than treating intention as sufficient.#Not the best answer. Seeing similar others perform the behaviour successfully can strengthen self-efficacy and outcome expectations.
}

::HC-W2-M11::Tuberculosis patients value treatment but stop attending when transport costs rise. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=opportunity rather than motivation#Correct. A motivated person can still be blocked by physical opportunity.
~compatibility in diffusion of innovations#Not the best answer. A motivated person can still be blocked by physical opportunity.
~perceived behavioural control#Not the best answer. A motivated person can still be blocked by physical opportunity.
~poor theory fit#Not the best answer. A motivated person can still be blocked by physical opportunity.
}

::HC-W2-M12::Tuberculosis patients value treatment but stop attending when transport costs rise. What is the strongest next response? {
=Investigate transport support, decentralised refill options or community delivery before intensifying motivational messaging.#Correct. A motivated person can still be blocked by physical opportunity.
~Explain how the new recommendation fits with familiar routines and give a simple schedule comparison.#Not the best answer. A motivated person can still be blocked by physical opportunity.
~Add negotiation skills, partner-level approaches and supportive services rather than treating intention as sufficient.#Not the best answer. A motivated person can still be blocked by physical opportunity.
~Start with behavioural evidence, identify determinants and then select the theory or combination that best explains the problem.#Not the best answer. A motivated person can still be blocked by physical opportunity.
}

::HC-W2-M13::Parents are uncertain about a new vaccine because it differs from the immunisation schedule they already know. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=compatibility in diffusion of innovations#Correct. Perceived fit with existing practices influences adoption.
~perceived behavioural control#Not the best answer. Perceived fit with existing practices influences adoption.
~poor theory fit#Not the best answer. Perceived fit with existing practices influences adoption.
~reverse causality in cross-sectional theory measures#Not the best answer. Perceived fit with existing practices influences adoption.
}

::HC-W2-M14::Parents are uncertain about a new vaccine because it differs from the immunisation schedule they already know. What is the strongest next response? {
=Explain how the new recommendation fits with familiar routines and give a simple schedule comparison.#Correct. Perceived fit with existing practices influences adoption.
~Add negotiation skills, partner-level approaches and supportive services rather than treating intention as sufficient.#Not the best answer. Perceived fit with existing practices influences adoption.
~Start with behavioural evidence, identify determinants and then select the theory or combination that best explains the problem.#Not the best answer. Perceived fit with existing practices influences adoption.
~Treat the association cautiously and use longitudinal or experimental evidence before making a causal mechanism claim.#Not the best answer. Perceived fit with existing practices influences adoption.
}

::HC-W2-M15::A campaign raises intention to use condoms, but participants report they cannot negotiate use with partners. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=perceived behavioural control#Correct. Intention may not translate to behaviour when control is limited.
~poor theory fit#Not the best answer. Intention may not translate to behaviour when control is limited.
~reverse causality in cross-sectional theory measures#Not the best answer. Intention may not translate to behaviour when control is limited.
~weak mechanism measurement#Not the best answer. Intention may not translate to behaviour when control is limited.
}

::HC-W2-M16::A campaign raises intention to use condoms, but participants report they cannot negotiate use with partners. What is the strongest next response? {
=Add negotiation skills, partner-level approaches and supportive services rather than treating intention as sufficient.#Correct. Intention may not translate to behaviour when control is limited.
~Start with behavioural evidence, identify determinants and then select the theory or combination that best explains the problem.#Not the best answer. Intention may not translate to behaviour when control is limited.
~Treat the association cautiously and use longitudinal or experimental evidence before making a causal mechanism claim.#Not the best answer. Intention may not translate to behaviour when control is limited.
~Measure perceived norms or referent approval directly and relate them to the intended behaviour.#Not the best answer. Intention may not translate to behaviour when control is limited.
}
